akr 0.1
project lege-ecosystem

record lege-ecosystem.decision.a-fold-may-not-change-a-letters-counters/1 : decision {
    title "Truetyping never folds shapes that enclose different counters, and a stroke is far even when it is a hairline"
    state proposed
    scope [ path "lege-process/encoding/bitrows.rs", path "lege-process/encoding/glyphfont.rs" ]
    decision """
        Two gates now guard the glyph dictionary against folding one letter into another. First, topology: a shape's enclosed counters are counted (`holes`, a flood fill from the border) and no match is considered across a different count — `c` never becomes `e`, `h` never becomes `b`, `a` never becomes `u`. The count is only trusted where the strokes have an interior, because a wall one pixel thick is opened or closed by a single speck. Second, the strict gate gained the far-pixel test the tolerant gate already had: ink that has none of the other shape's within a pixel of it, capped at 1% of the ink (minimum 2) with no far group larger than 2. A change of impression puts its difference along the strokes it thickens, so it is never far; a stroke one shape has and the other lacks is far along its length, however thin it is. Strict matches are also ranked by that structural agreement first, so rejecting a candidate never hands the instance to a worse one that survived.
        """
    context """
        A user's own run of the ITU T.88 specification came back with `which` as `wbicb`, `the` as `tbe`, `decoded` as `deeoded`, `are` as `ure` — whole letters replaced, in body text, in the format that had just been made the default. The fold dump showed why: the tolerant gate forgives ink within a pixel of the other shape, and at reading sizes the crossbar of an `e` is four pixels long with both ends touching the bowl, so only two pixels are left to notice. `far_blob <= 3` was meant to catch a missing stroke and cannot, because a stroke at that size is not three pixels of anything. The strict gate had no far test at all: its 2x2-block measure sees nothing in a one-pixel hairline, so it merged `a` with `e` and `u` with `n` on a heavier face.
        """
    consequences """
        Dictionaries grow: 3 pages of Gobineau 150 to 160 shapes, 5 pages of T.88 372 to 414, 5 pages of the Gernet scan 443 to 465 — 3-10%, and the fonts with them. Splitting is the safe direction: two prototypes for one letter cost bytes, one prototype for two letters costs the reader the word. Counters are computed per component (a flood fill alongside the ink profile) and prune candidates, so the tolerant stage got faster rather than slower. A scan whose counter is filled in by heavy ink now keeps its own prototype instead of folding; that is a split, not a substitution. Text on a source that was already binarized at low resolution still merges touching pairs (`in` reads as `m`), which no pixel budget separates at ten pixels a letter.
        """
    claim a-hairline-stroke-is-far-not-thick {
        text """
            The 2x2-block `thick` measure cannot see a one-pixel stroke, which is why the strict gate merged `a` with `e`; the far-pixel measure sees it as a connected run along the stroke's length and is now bounded for both gates.
            """
    }
    claim counters-are-identity {
        text """
            A letter's enclosed counters survive every change of weight, so a match across a different count is always wrong; `find_match` refuses it whenever both shapes have stroke interiors.
            """
    }
    claim rejection-alone-does-not-make-matching-safer {
        text """
            The search keeps the best candidate it is offered, so narrowing a filter that can exclude the *right* prototype (the dimension slack, or a far bound tight enough to reject a broken edge) hands the instance to the next best wrong one instead. Filters that are semantic (a different letter) help; filters that are about quality (a damaged instance) must rank rather than reject.
            """
    }
    derived_from [ @lege-ecosystem.work.glyph-font-text-output ]
}

record lege-ecosystem.decision.sideways-pages-are-turned-upright-with-rotate/1 : decision {
    title "A sideways page is turned upright with /Rotate, never on its head"
    state proposed
    scope [
        path "lege-pdf/write/src/artifact.rs",
        path "lege-pdf/write/src/pages.rs",
        path "lege-process/core/pdf_artifact.rs",
        path "lege-process/pipeline/pdf_tokio_pipeline.rs"
    ]
    decision """
        The PDF writer emits `/Rotate` on the page dictionary, and Lege sets it from the quarter turns the orientation detector already measures, so a page scanned sideways opens upright. Only the sideways turns (90 and 270) are acted on; a 180 reading is ignored. Nothing is resampled and no coordinate changes: the raster, the OCR text layer and the glyph text keep the space they were written in and turn together.
        """
    context """
        Orientation detection has shipped since pass 6 and drives OCR (a sideways CJK scan went from 27 to about 1100 words a page), but the output was never turned: `straighten.rs` puts every glyph back where it was scanned. The user made the GUI default conditional on sideways pages being flippable in production, so the flip had to become real rather than possible. The detector calls a page sideways only when the turned page's baselines are half again as sharp as the page's own, but calls it upside down on a margin of 1.15, which is a much weaker signal.
        """
    consequences """
        A page whose orientation nothing measured is left alone: the frame is computed for every page under truetyping, but only when OCR is on in the other text formats. `/Rotate` is written per page, never inherited from `/Pages`, so a book may mix orientations. DjVu has no equivalent attribute and would need the raster itself turned, so DjVu output is unchanged. The requested height was applied to the sideways render, so an upright page comes out taller than the device asked for.
        """
    claim no-180-turn {
        text """
            Turns of 2 are dropped rather than written: the upside-down margin is 1.15 against the sideways 1.5, and a page wrongly stood on its head is worse for an e-ink reader than one left as scanned.
            """
    }
    claim rotate-from-quarter-turns {
        text """
            `PageFrame.turns` counts clockwise quarter turns that make the text upright, which is exactly the `/Rotate` convention, so the value passes through unchanged (`core/pdf_artifact.rs::display_rotation`).
            """
    }
    derived_from [ @lege-ecosystem.work.glyph-font-text-output ]
}

record lege-ecosystem.decision.top-level-application-license-agpl/1 : decision {
    title "License the top-level Lege applications under AGPL-3.0-only"
    state active
    scope [
        path "Cargo.toml",
        path "LICENSE",
        path "NOTICE",
        path "README.md",
        path "lege-android/**",
        path "lege-misc/docs/licenses.html",
        path "lege-process/**",
        path "lege-viewer/**"
    ]
    topic top-level-application-license
    decision """
        The Lege top-level workspace and combined application products are licensed AGPL-3.0-only, authorizing direct linking to the AGPL-3.0-only DJVULibRust crate. The root and application package metadata and included license texts must say AGPL-3.0-only. Independently usable codec and third-party subtrees retain their explicit licenses: DJVULibRust remains AGPL-3.0-only, while jbig2enc-rust and jp2lam remain MIT OR Apache-2.0.
        """
    context """
        The user selected the plan's recommended D1 outcome: everything is AGPL again at the top level. The former MIT application declaration existed to support the now-retired arms-length subprocess boundary.
        """
    consequences """
        Lege may restore the in-process DJVULibRust API. Application crates that directly link lege-process, including lege-android, must not continue to claim MIT. Mixed-license subtrees and third-party notices remain explicitly mapped rather than being relabeled.
        """
    derived_from [ @lege-ecosystem.work.open-source-monorepo-relaunch/1 ]
    supported_by [
        @lege-ecosystem.evidence.djvu-license-linkage-boundary-audit-20260822/1,
        @lege-ecosystem.evidence.djvu-subprocess-license-surface-audit-20260822/1
    ]
}

record lege-ecosystem.decision.truetyping-banks-fonts-and-respects-a-bilevel-source/1 : decision {
    title "A document may carry several glyph fonts, and a book already black and white is traced at its own resolution"
    state proposed
    scope [
        path "lege-pdf/read/src/session.rs",
        path "lege-pdf/write/src/**",
        path "lege-process/encoding/glyphfont.rs",
        path "lege-process/pipeline/config.rs",
        path "lege-process/pipeline/pdf_tokio_pipeline.rs"
    ]
    decision """
        Two changes take the ceiling off long books. A TrueType font holds 65536 glyph ids, so the session now keeps a dictionary per *bank*: a page whose components would carry the current dictionary past that limit starts the next one, every page draws from exactly one font, and the writer emits `/F2`, `/F3`, … with a reserved id per bank. And a page whose ink is already one bit — a full-page stencil, or a full-page image with a one-bit soft mask — has no more detail to give at any scale, so truetyping is rendered at that resolution rather than at its own working height. A greyscale or colour scan keeps the raised height: its soft edges resolve *better* the finer they are sampled.
        """
    context """
        An 836-page book failed after about seven minutes with `PDF writer actor has stopped`. It was the 16-bit glyph id space: the page adapter rejects an id past 65535 and the writer actor died on it, while the real message was lost because the sender only sees a closed channel. The same run was slow for a second reason — the book was itself a Lege output, a greyscale JP2 under a JBIG2 stencil at 1200 px, and rendering it at 2400 invented interpolated edges that no two impressions of a letter shared: 120 pages made 24 381 glyphs in 3 minutes at 2400 px and 9 363 in 18 seconds at 1400.
        """
    consequences """
        The book now finishes in 3 minutes with two fonts. The writer actor records what it died of before dropping its receiver, so a page worker reports the cause rather than the symptom. Bank assignment is decided before a page is analysed, from the component count plus headroom, so a page never straddles two fonts and no composite ever crosses one. `GlyphFontSession::with_prototype_cap` exists so the split can be tested without 65 000 shapes.
        """
    claim bilevel-sources-have-a-ceiling {
        text """
            `CompiledDocumentPage::bilevel_raster_height` reports the full-page-equivalent height of a page's one-bit ink layer (the stencil itself, or a one-bit soft mask) and nothing for a continuous-tone page, and the pipeline lowers the truetyping working height to it, never below the height the caller asked for.
            """
    }
    claim one-font-per-page {
        text """
            `PreparedGlyphLayer::font` names the bank a page's glyph ids index; the writer reserves one Type0 id per bank and writes each font at finalize, so a page never draws from two.
            """
    }
    claim the-actor-says-what-it-died-of {
        text """
            The writer actor stores its error in a `OnceLock` the handle shares before it drops the receiver, so a send that finds the channel closed reports `PDF writer failed: <cause>` instead of `PDF writer actor has stopped`.
            """
    }
    derived_from [ @lege-ecosystem.work.glyph-font-text-output ]
}

record lege-ecosystem.decision.truetyping-is-the-default-pdf-text-format/1 : decision {
    title "Truetyping is the name and the default for PDF text output"
    state proposed
    scope [
        path "lege-process/GUI/Freya/**",
        path "lege-process/core/main.rs",
        path "lege-process/language_service/**",
        path "lege-process/pipeline/config.rs"
    ]
    decision """
        The glyph-font text format is called `truetyping` in every user-facing surface and is the default PDF text encoder in the Freya GUI. `glyphfont` stays an accepted spelling of the CLI and config value forever, normalized at parse time, so scripted runs and saved settings keep working; the Rust module, the log prefixes and the AKR history keep the name they were built under, because they name the mechanism rather than the product. In the GUI the two opt-outs are checkboxes that exclude each other by construction: `CompressionType` gained a `Truetyping` variant and is now the single field the text encoder is read from, so JBIG2 and compatibility mode (CCITT4 + baseline JPEG) cannot both be on, and clearing either returns to truetyping.
        """
    context """
        Seven passes brought the format to parity: on a 20-page scan it is 183 KB against JBIG2 symbol mode's 378 KB at equal or better legibility, and the user signed off on the rendered outputs. The old `effective_text_format` derived the encoder from `image_processing_type` whenever layout analysis was on, so the GUI's real default was CCITT4 and the compatibility checkbox's `compression_type` flip was a no-op on the default path. Making the text encoder an explicit field removes that coupling: how image regions are treated no longer decides how text is written.
        """
    consequences """
        Dithered image regions no longer imply JBIG2 text, so the JBIG2 halftone toggle needs its own gate on the text encoder (it was relying on the coupling) in both the panel and the argument builder. Raster reflow re-renders every page as an image and has no text encoder, so it refuses truetyping on the CLI and is not offered it in the GUI. The Freya GUI is the only frontend changed: the sheet-music GUI and lege-viewer carry their own copies of the option model and keep the old default.
        """
    claim image-handling-no-longer-picks-the-encoder {
        text """
            `effective_text_format` no longer consults `image_processing_type` or `layout_analysis`; before this change Original meant CCITT4 and Dithered meant JBIG2 whenever layout analysis was on, which made CCITT4 the GUI's actual default.
            """
    }
    claim one-field-decides-the-encoder {
        text """
            The Freya GUI reads the PDF text encoder from `CompressionType` alone (`Truetyping | Jbig2 | Ccitt4`); `ProcessingOptions::set_text_encoder` keeps `jpeg_compat` and the JBIG2 sub-options in step, which is what makes the two checkboxes mutually exclusive.
            """
    }
    claim truetyping-is-canonical {
        text """
            `truetyping` is the canonical text_format value; `glyphfont` is normalized to it in `PipelineConfig::set_text_format` and in the CLI's `--text-format` parser, so the pipeline compares one constant (`pipeline::config::TRUETYPING`).
            """
    }
    derived_from [ @lege-ecosystem.work.glyph-font-text-output ]
}
